<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP becomes pale and sickly if it cannot provide aid to others.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm a little hesitant about this, and this kind of thing in general: it's usually less interesting to me if you add anomalous properties to a SCP that force the Foundation to let it do things than if you have a good narrative reason for the Foundation to let it do things, y'know?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After intense review, the SCP was allowed to accompany scientists and help within the site it is located at.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm kinda hesitant about this because a large part of the idea here is that the SCP tries to be helpful and isn't?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I want to keep the focus of the article on the mysteries surrounding the SCP and its desire to connect with members of the Foundation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What is this mystery? This feels like the it's meant to be the core of the article but it also don't particularly feel like something interesting to explore; but I don't really know how you're like&#8230;intending exploring this? Like, how are you setting this mystery up to the reader? How are you getting the reader invested in it's outcome? How are you resolving the mystery?</p>
<hr />
<p>My concern is that it feels like the most interesting part of this is the bit between the SCP Allison joining the foundation and them discovering the Thumbling, and the thumbling growing into becoming an actually valuable member of the Foundation, and like, the story of their growth; and it feels like you've not really&#8230;covered that section at all?</p>
</div>
