<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> A semi-sentient liquid metal that bonds with a persons nervous system and tries to protect that person from harm, but ultimately does more harm than good.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The SCP is an unknown liquid metal that enters a persons body through the skin, bonding with that persons nervous system. Whenever an object/person comes into contact with the skin of the host, the metal will rush through the persons nerves to the point of impact, surfacing out of the skin to form a shield that protects the person from physical trauma. The SCP accidentally damages the nerves when it travels through them, causing intense pain in the host in the short run, and loss of fine motor skills to loss of all feelings in limbs in the long run. Even if the SCP is inactive in the host, over time the host will develop symptoms similar to long-term mercury poisoning, which will result in death after 10-15 months.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The SCP will only have one host at a time, and will not transfer to another while the previous host still lives. It initially transfers by skin to skin contact with the previous hosts corpse, however if the SCP is without a host for an extended period of time, it will leave the previous hosts body and seek out the nearest human to bond with. If it is unable to find a host after leaving the previous host, it will become increasingly aggressive and stronger to break out and locate a new host.</p>
<p>Due to the SCP reacting to even the smallest contacts with the skin, it becomes nigh impossible for the host to complete small tasks, and even a small shift in their clothing can set of the SCP. Monitoring the effects the SCP has on its hosts is extremely difficult, because the SCP prevents the foundation from taking any samples while the host still lives, and most forms of scans are ineffective.</p>
<p>In order to contain the anomaly, the foundation will expose a D-class in a medically induced coma to the SCP, and try to keep them alive for as long as possible. After the death of the D-class, a new D-class will be put into a coma before transferring the SCP to them.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
<strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> Something created to protect humans ends up damaging them in the long run.
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> Linked to the broken god.</p>

